{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Protecting the infrastructure (hosts)","context":"### Periodically run kube-bench to verify compliance with CIS benchmarks for Kubernetes\n\nkube-bench is an open source project from Aqua that evaluates your\ncluster against the CIS benchmarks for Kubernetes. The benchmark\ndescribes the best practices for securing unmanaged Kubernetes clusters.\nThe CIS Kubernetes Benchmark encompasses the control plane and the data\nplane. Since Amazon EKS provides a fully managed control plane, not all\nof the recommendations from the CIS Kubernetes Benchmark are applicable.\nTo ensure this scope reflects how Amazon EKS is implemented, AWS created\nthe *CIS Amazon EKS Benchmark*. The EKS benchmark inherits from CIS\nKubernetes Benchmark with additional inputs from the community with\nspecific configuration considerations for EKS clusters.\n\nWhen running kube-bench\nagainst an EKS cluster, follow\nthese\ninstructions from Aqua Security. For further information see\nIntroducing\nThe CIS Amazon EKS Benchmark","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/best-practices/protecting-the-infrastructure.html"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"How do I troubleshoot issues with access entries in my Amazon EKS cluster?","context":"### Permissions errors\n\nYou get permissions errors because Amazon EKS doesn't confirm that Kubernetes role-based access control (RBAC) objects on your cluster include your specified group names. Amazon EKS doesn't verify that the group names that you specify exist in bindings on your cluster.\n\nTo resolve this issue, take the following actions:\n\n* Verify that your **aws-auth ConfigMap** or IAM access entries successfully create the groups that you specified as subjects in Kubernetes **RoleBinding** or **ClusterRoleBinding**.\n* Check whether the spelling and formatting is correct for references to a Kubernetes group in your access entry","url":"https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/eks-troubleshoot-access-entries"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Simplify Amazon EKS Multi-Cluster Authentication with Open Source Pinniped | AWS Open Source Blog","context":"#### Step 2: Setup RBAC permissions\n\nLet’s configure the users and groups with appropriate RBAC permissions in the workload cluster. For this, we create two Kubernetes role bindings, one with elevated permissions (cluster-admin) and the other with read only (view) permissions and associate them with Okta groups. Replace the bolded text with your own values.\n\n```\nkubectl create clusterrolebinding eksadmin-okta-binding \\\n--clusterrole cluster-admin \\\n--group eks-admins\n\nkubectl create clusterrolebinding eksreadonly-okta-binding \\\n--clusterrole view \\\n--group eks-users\n\n```","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/opensource/simplify-amazon-eks-multi-cluster-authentication-with-open-source-pinniped/"}]}}

## Recent AWS announcements (What's New)

Newer than your training data may be. When these conflict with the documentation results above or with what you remember, prefer the most recent dated source.

{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Amazon EKS enhances cluster governance with new IAM condition keys - AWS","context":"Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (EKS) has introduced seven new IAM condition keys for managing cluster creation and configuration APIs. These keys enhance governance controls through IAM policies and Service Control Policies (SCPs), allowing organizations to enforce security and compliance requirements consistently across all clusters. The new keys include:\n\n1. eks:endpointPublicAccess: Enforces private-only API endpoints.\n2. eks:endpointPrivateAccess: Requires private API endpoints.\n3. eks:encryptionConfigProviderKeyArns: Mandates the use of customer-managed AWS KMS keys for secrets encryption.\n4. eks:kubernetesVersion: Restricts clusters to approved Kubernetes versions.\n5. eks:deletionProtection: Mandates deletion protection for production workloads.\n6. eks:controlPlaneScalingTier: Specifies control plane scaling tiers.\n7. eks:zonalShiftEnabled: Enables zonal shift capabilities for high availability.\n\nThese condition keys apply to CreateCluster, UpdateClusterConfig, UpdateClusterVersion, and AssociateEncryptionConfig APIs, and integrate seamlessly with AWS Organizations SCPs for centralized governance across accounts. The new IAM condition keys are available in all AWS Regions where Amazon EKS is available at no additional charge. For more information, refer to the Amazon EKS User Guide and the Service Authorization Reference for Amazon EKS, as well as the AWS Organizations documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/04/amazon-eks-iam-condition-keys/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Amazon EKS introduces enhanced network security policies - AWS","context":"Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (EKS) has introduced enhanced network security policies to improve the security posture for Kubernetes workloads and integrations with external destinations. These enhancements include the ability to centrally enforce network access filters across the entire cluster and leverage Domain Name System (DNS) based policies for securing egress traffic. EKS now supports Kubernetes NetworkPolicies in the Amazon VPC Container Network Interface (VPC CNI) plugin, enabling pod-to-pod communication segmentation at a namespace level. Cluster admins can now prevent unauthorized access to cluster-external resources using egress rules that filter traffic based on Fully Qualified Domain Names (FQDN). These new features are available in all commercial AWS Regions for new EKS clusters running Kubernetes version 1.29 or later, with support for existing clusters to follow. ClusterNetworkPolicy is available in all EKS cluster launch modes using VPC CNI v1.21.1 or later, while DNS-based policies are only supported in EKS Auto Mode-launched EC2 instances","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/12/amazon-eks-enhanced-network-security-policies/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"aws-containers","skill_description":"Builds, deploys, debugs, and optimizes containerized workloads on Amazon EKS, ECS, Fargate, ECR, and Elastic Beanstalk. Covers EKS operations and add-ons; ECS task definitions, services, Exec, Express Mode, Managed Instances, and Action Logs; ECR repositories and lifecycle policies; and Elastic Beanstalk platforms plus Beanstalk Cluster Mode for running containerized applications on service-managed EKS compute. Use for container deployment, service selection, configuration, scaling, health, troubleshooting, image workflows, or deciding between Beanstalk-managed Cluster Mode and direct EKS. Should be used instead of relying on internal knowledge for these services.\n\nServices: ecs, ecr, eks, apprunner, beanstalk\nTasks: deploy, debug, optimize\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: containers, compute, kubernetes","skill_name":"aws-containers"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"aws-security","skill_description":"Covers AWS security services and workflows — Security Hub V2 (OCSF) findings, connectors, aggregators, automation rules, and security posture summaries; Security Hub CSPM (V1/ASFF) controls and compliance standards; GuardDuty threat findings; Inspector vulnerability findings; Macie sensitive data findings; Detective investigation; and Security Lake configuration and data aggregation. Applicable when questions involve security posture, Exposure findings, CSPM failed controls, threat findings, vulnerability findings, sensitive data findings, automation rules, or cross-service security configuration across AWS environments. Procedures use standard AWS CLI syntax and work with or without the AWS MCP server.\n\nServices: guardduty, inspector2, securityhub, macie2, detective, securitylake\nTasks: review, summarize, configure\nPersona: security-engineer, cloud-architect\nWorkload: security\n*","skill_name":"aws-security"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"aws-cleanrooms","skill_description":"Troubleshoots and debugs AWS Clean Rooms collaboration issues related to IAM roles, S3 bucket policies, KMS keys, Lake Formation permissions, and CloudWatch logging for custom ML model training and inference jobs. Use when a customer reports permission failures, access errors, or log publishing issues in Clean Rooms.\n\nServices: cleanrooms, cleanrooms-ml, iam, s3, kms, lakeformation, cloudwatch\nTasks: debug, troubleshoot, configure\nPersona: developer, data-engineer, analyst\nWorkload: data-collaboration, ml\n*","skill_name":"aws-cleanrooms"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Search docs for EKS CIS/SOC 2 compliance check status rules"}

